From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: allwinner: A733 minimal — power the on-board WiFi/BT module (PM0/PM1)

The on-board WiFi/BT module never enumerates on our image. The hub in front of
it does: the CH334F (U9) comes up on USB1/EHCI1, reports four ports, and every
port is empty. A device that is not powered never asserts its D+ pull-up, so
the hub has nothing to report — which is exactly the symptom.

The module has its own rail and it is not the hub's VBUS. DCDC1 feeds a WPM2015
load switch (Q8) whose output is WIFI_3V3, and the module sits on hub port 1 as
a soldered device rather than behind a connector. Two AON pins gate it:

  PM0 = USB_WIFI_PWR
  PM1 = WL-REG-ON

Silicon agrees with the spec: PM_CFG0 @0x07025030 reads 0x001ff111, so PM0 and
PM1 are muxed to output.

Before this patch the tree drove exactly two AON GPIOs — PL2 (`arm64-dts-a733-add-usb3-dwc3-combophy`, the Type-C
J4 VBUS) and PM5 (`arm64-dts-a733-aon-pinctrl-usb-host-vbus`, the J8 downstream-port VBUS) — and neither reaches this
module. The gap had been noticed but left open. `arm64-dts-a733-aon-pinctrl-usb-host-vbus`'s own note that PM5 also powers
the WiFi/BT module is superseded: PM5
switches the downstream port VBUS, not this rail.

These are real load-switch outputs gated by a SoC pin, not the regulator-fixed
placeholder trap — nothing here stands in for a switchable PMIC rail; the switch
is physically there and its enable is the pin.

PM0's node is placed before PM1's so the regulator core registers power before
reset-release, the order the reference board's own device tree uses.

Deliberately omitted: regulator-boot-on, which would assert a bootloader state
nobody here has measured, and regulator-enable-ramp-delay, which has no documented
value to base it on (no WPM2015 datasheet is in the tree) and is inert while
nothing consumes the rails. regulator-always-on is what holds them, exactly as
for usb-host-vbus in `arm64-dts-a733-aon-pinctrl-usb-host-vbus`.

HW proof pending: the module must appear behind the hub in lsusb, and a PM0/PM1
toggle must produce a disconnect/reconnect — the standard `arm64-dts-a733-aon-pinctrl-usb-host-vbus` met. This patch
does NOT claim a working wlan0: that needs a wireless driver and is a separate
change with its own proof.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -520,6 +520,47 @@
 	};
 
 	/*
+	 * WIFI_3V3 -- the on-board WiFi/BT module's own supply. It is NOT the hub
+	 * VBUS: DCDC1 feeds a WPM2015 load switch (Q8) whose output is WIFI_3V3,
+	 * and the module hangs off hub port 1 as a soldered device, not a
+	 * connector. PM5 above switches only the *downstream port*
+	 * VBUS for J8
+	 * and does not reach this module.
+	 *
+	 * Two pins gate it, both on the AON S_GPIO bank (r_pio, bank index 1 = PM):
+	 *   PM0 = USB_WIFI_PWR
+	 *   PM1 = WL-REG-ON
+	 * Silicon agrees: PM_CFG0 @0x07025030 reads 0x001ff111, i.e. PM0/PM1 are
+	 * muxed to output.
+	 *
+	 * Power first, then release reset -- so PM0's node comes before PM1's, the
+	 * order the regulator core registers them in.
+	 *
+	 * Neither node carries regulator-boot-on: that would assert a bootloader
+	 * state nobody here has measured. regulator-always-on alone is what holds
+	 * them, exactly as for usb-host-vbus above.
+	 */
+	reg_wifi_pwr: regulator-wifi-pwr {
+		compatible = "regulator-fixed";
+		regulator-name = "wifi-pwr";
+		regulator-min-microvolt = <3300000>;
+		regulator-max-microvolt = <3300000>;
+		gpio = <&r_pio 1 0 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		enable-active-high;
+		regulator-always-on;
+	};
+
+	reg_wifi_regon: regulator-wifi-regon {
+		compatible = "regulator-fixed";
+		regulator-name = "wifi-regon";
+		regulator-min-microvolt = <3300000>;
+		regulator-max-microvolt = <3300000>;
+		gpio = <&r_pio 1 1 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		enable-active-high;
+		regulator-always-on;
+	};
+
+	/*
 	 * VBUS for the Type-C J4 SuperSpeed port, switched by SGM2576 U2 whose
 	 * enable is USB0-DRVVBUS = PL2 (the "USB0" net name is a schematic label;
 	 * on page-12 it gates J4's VBUS switch).
